Hi Pat,

We ran a short survey of members and visitors last month about the front desk=
, and the kiosk
came up a lot. I thought you'd like to see what people said, good and bad, an=
d there's one
question at the end.

Who signs in. Members use their own fob at the turnstile, so the kiosk is onl=
y for visitors:
personal trainers who work freelance here, guests members bring along, physio=
s who rent our
treatment room, and the odd delivery driver. On a weekday that's 40 to 60 peo=
ple.

The good. Most people found it quick the first time. The large buttons were m=
entioned several
times, and two of the physios said they liked getting a badge with their room=
 number on it.
Reception staff said they spend far less time on the phone tracking down who =
is in the building,
and our duty managers like seeing the list on the dashboard during a fire dri=
ll.

The not so good. The main complaint, by a long way, came from people who visi=
t often. Our
freelance trainers come in five or six times a week, and they have to type th=
eir name, company
and who they're visiting every single time. Several said it feels like being =
treated as a
stranger in a place where they practically work. One trainer now walks past t=
he kiosk and signs
the paper book we kept for emergencies, which defeats the point.

Smaller things. A few people asked for the screen to be brighter, as it sits =
opposite the big
window. One guest couldn't find the "visiting a member" option. Someone sugge=
sted a coffee
machine next to it, which I'll take as a compliment, and a regular asked for =
a bench beside it
for people taking their shoes off.

Numbers. Over the four weeks of the survey, the kiosk signed in 1,180 visits.=
 Freelance
trainers made up about a third of them, which surprised us. Guests of members=
 were next, then the
physios. We had 64 survey replies in all, 22 of them from trainers, so their =
view carries a lot
of weight in the results. Most of the trainers said they'd be happy to keep u=
sing the kiosk if
signing in took a few seconds rather than a minute.

Our staff. The front-desk team would like the regular visitors sorted out mor=
e than anything
else. They also asked whether the badge could show how long the visitor is bo=
oked for, so they
know when a room should be free, but that's a nice-to-have.

Next year. We're hoping to open a small studio next door in the spring, with =
its own entrance.
I'll be in touch about that separately when it's firmer.

Thanks again for a system that has, on the whole, made our front desk calmer.

Here's the question, then: is there a quicker way for regular visitors to sig=
n in, such as
scanning a code, without typing their details each time?

